<p class="gna-lede">Blepharoplasty is surgery to remove or reposition excess skin and fat in the upper or lower eyelids. Upper eyelid surgery helps hooding that makes the eyes look tired or, in some people, rests on the lashes and narrows vision; lower eyelid surgery reduces under-eye bags. Most people are back to normal activities in about two weeks, but it refreshes the eyes rather than stopping ageing, and it does not remove dark circles caused by pigmentation.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Upper blepharoplasty treats excess, hooded upper-lid skin; lower blepharoplasty treats bags under the eyes.</li>
    <li>Hooding that blocks vision is a functional problem; a tired appearance is a cosmetic one.</li>
    <li>A droopy lid margin (ptosis), low brows, dark circles and hollows need different treatments.</li>
    <li>Dry, gritty eyes are common for a few weeks; existing dry eye should be assessed before surgery.</li>
    <li>Bruising usually fades over two to three weeks; scars and final results settle over several months.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="what-is-blepharoplasty">What is blepharoplasty?</h2>
<p>With age, eyelid skin loses elasticity and stretches, and the thin membrane that holds back the fat cushion around the eye weakens, allowing fat to bulge forwards. In the upper lids this creates a fold of skin that hangs over the crease, often called hooding. In the lower lids it creates bags that cast shadows. Genetics play a large part, which is why some people have prominent under-eye bags in their twenties or thirties.</p>
<p>Blepharoplasty removes the excess skin, and removes, reduces or repositions the bulging fat. It can be done on the upper lids, the lower lids, or both, and is sometimes combined with other procedures such as a brow lift.</p>

<h2 id="upper-vs-lower">Upper vs lower blepharoplasty</h2>
<h3>Upper eyelid surgery</h3>
<p>The surgeon marks the natural crease of the eyelid, removes a measured strip of skin above it, and, where needed, a small amount of fat or muscle. The incision is closed with fine stitches, so the scar sits in the crease, where it is hidden when the eyes are open. Upper blepharoplasty is often done under local anaesthesia, with or without sedation, and takes around an hour.</p>
<h3>Lower eyelid surgery</h3>
<p>Lower eyelid surgery is more complex. If the main problem is bulging fat and the skin is in good condition, the surgeon can work through the inside of the eyelid (a transconjunctival approach), leaving no skin scar. If there is also excess skin, the incision is made just below the lashes. Fat may be removed, or repositioned into the hollow beneath the bags to give a smoother transition into the cheek. If the lower lid is lax, it may be tightened at the outer corner to keep it in position.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Upper blepharoplasty</th><th>Lower blepharoplasty</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What it treats</td><td>Excess, hooded upper-lid skin; fat bulges near the inner corner</td><td>Under-eye bags; loose lower-lid skin</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Incision</td><td>In the natural lid crease</td><td>Inside the lid, or just below the lashes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Anaesthesia</td><td>Often local, with or without sedation</td><td>Local with sedation, or general</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical social recovery</td><td>About 10–14 days</td><td>About 2 weeks, sometimes longer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Particular risks</td><td>Difficulty closing the eye if too much skin is removed</td><td>Lower lid pulling down or turning outwards</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="functional-vs-cosmetic">Functional or cosmetic?</h2>
<p>Blepharoplasty is considered <strong>functional</strong> when excess upper-lid skin rests on the lashes and blocks the upper or outer part of your vision. People with this problem often find themselves raising their eyebrows all day to see, which can cause forehead ache and tiredness by evening. A visual field test, done with the lids in their natural position and then taped up, documents how much vision the skin is blocking.</p>
<p>It is considered <strong>cosmetic</strong> when the aim is to look less tired or reduce puffiness and bags, without an effect on sight. Both are legitimate reasons to seek advice, but they are treated differently by insurers. Cosmetic eyelid surgery is generally excluded by Indian health insurance policies. When vision is documented to be blocked, some insurers may consider upper-lid surgery, subject to policy terms and pre-authorisation, but approval is not assured.</p>

<h2 id="not-the-eyelid">When the problem is not the eyelid skin</h2>
<p>A careful examination matters because several conditions look like "droopy eyelids" but need different treatment:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ptosis:</strong> the edge of the eyelid itself sits too low, because the muscle that lifts it has stretched or weakened, which is common with age and long-term contact-lens wear. This needs a ptosis repair rather than skin removal, though the two are sometimes combined.</li>
  <li><strong>Low brows:</strong> when the eyebrows have descended, they push skin down onto the lids. Removing eyelid skin alone can pull the brows even lower, so a brow lift may be the better first step. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/brow-lift-vs-botox-droopy-brows/">brow lift vs Botox for droopy brows</a> explains the options, and you can read about <a href="/brow-lift-treatment">brow lift treatment</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Dark circles:</strong> often due to pigmentation, which is common in Indian skin, or thin skin over visible blood vessels. Surgery does not change pigmentation.</li>
  <li><strong>Hollows under the eyes:</strong> a tear trough hollow may respond better to fat repositioning or carefully placed <a href="/dermal-fillers-hyderabad">dermal fillers</a> than to fat removal.</li>
  <li><strong>Crow's feet and fine lines:</strong> these are caused by muscle movement and skin ageing, and are usually treated with <a href="/botox-treatment">Botox injections</a> or skin treatments rather than surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>Puffiness that varies from day to day:</strong> allergies, thyroid problems, kidney conditions, poor sleep and salty food can all cause it, and should be considered first.</li>
</ul>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>See a doctor urgently</strong> if one eyelid droops suddenly, especially with double vision, a severe headache, or a pupil that looks larger than the other, or if the droop worsens as the day goes on. These can be signs of a nerve or muscle condition that needs prompt medical assessment, not cosmetic treatment.</p>
</div>

