<h2 id="dry-eye">Dry eye and other eye considerations</h2>
<p>The eyelids protect and lubricate the eye with every blink. After blepharoplasty, swelling and tightness mean the lids may not close fully for a short time, especially during sleep, and the eyes commonly feel dry, gritty or watery for a few weeks. This usually improves as swelling settles, helped by lubricating drops and ointment. Long-term dry eye after surgery is uncommon but possible.</p>
<p>The risk is higher if you already have dry eye, have had recent laser eye surgery, wear contact lenses, have thyroid eye disease, have had eyelid surgery before, or have lax lower lids. Long hours on screens, air-conditioned offices and dusty commutes can add to symptoms. Before surgery we ask about these, check your vision and tear film, and may ask an eye specialist (ophthalmologist) to examine your eyes. If there is pre-existing dry eye, skin is removed conservatively and the eye surface is treated first.</p>

<h2 id="recovery">How recovery goes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Day of surgery:</strong> most people go home the same day. Ointment may blur your vision for a few hours, so someone must take you home. Rest with your head raised and use cold compresses as advised.</li>
  <li><strong>Days 1–3:</strong> swelling and bruising are at their worst. The lids feel tight, may not close fully and may water. Take the medicines prescribed and avoid bending or lifting.</li>
  <li><strong>About one week:</strong> stitches, if not dissolvable, and supporting strips are usually removed.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 1–2:</strong> bruising fades; many people return to work at about two weeks. Screens are fine in moderation, though your eyes may tire quickly. Avoid contact lenses and eye make-up until your surgeon advises, often around two weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 2–4:</strong> light exercise usually resumes; strenuous activity a little later. Wear sunglasses outdoors to protect from sun, wind and dust.</li>
  <li><strong>Months 1–6:</strong> scars fade from pink to pale and the last swelling settles. The final result is usually judged at around three to six months.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not drive until your vision is clear and you are comfortable, and avoid smoking, which slows healing and irritates the eyes. Recovery varies between people and is usually longer when upper and lower lids are treated together.</p>

<h2 id="risks-and-expectations">Risks and realistic expectations</h2>
<p>Blepharoplasty is a commonly performed operation, but it carries risks that you should weigh carefully:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Dry, irritated eyes, and difficulty closing the eyes for a time</li>
  <li>Asymmetry between the two sides</li>
  <li>Visible or tight scars</li>
  <li>The lower lid pulling down or turning outwards (ectropion)</li>
  <li>Too little or too much tissue removed</li>
  <li>Temporary blurred or double vision, and altered sensation in the lids</li>
  <li>Rarely, injury to the eye muscles, bleeding behind the eye or loss of vision</li>
</ul>
<p>Bleeding behind the eye is rare and usually happens in the first day or two. Severe, increasing pain, a bulging eye or any loss of vision after eyelid surgery is an emergency: contact your surgeon immediately, or call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
<p>When the right patient has the right operation, blepharoplasty can give a brighter, less heavy appearance and, for functional cases, a wider field of vision. It will not change the basic shape of your eyes, remove pigmentation or stop ageing, and some people choose further treatment years later. A few patients need a revision to fine-tune the result.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Consider a consultation if heavy upper lids are affecting your vision or causing brow strain, or if bags or hooding bother you enough to consider surgery. A good assessment looks at the eyelids, the brows, the cheeks and the health of the eyes together, and should include an honest discussion of non-surgical alternatives.</p>
